      "writtenOn": "2017-01-28T00:18:35Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Just from layering perspective, I think we should stop using WifiStateMachine instance from WifiConnectivityManager. \n\nI would think of WifiStateMachine as the parent class and WifiConnectivityManager as a child class. It seems odd that WifiStateMachine and WifiConnectivityManager is calling each other. Maybe we can add some kind of callback mechanism which can be used by WificonnectivityManager to trigger connection requests to WifiStateMachine instead of passing the whole WifiStateMachine object to it?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 637,
        "startChar": 16,
        "endLine": 637,
        "endChar": 29
      },
      "revId": "21f7c746784993d3002f9a2da59575a70544ecfa",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "8e6b9e89_d900a24a",
        "filename": "service/java/com/android/server/wifi/WifiConnectivityManager.java",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 637,
      "author": {
        "id": 1086611
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-01-28T00:53:16Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Agreed. The situation today is definitely not ideal. It\u0027s the same issue as the rest of Wifi stack: the architecture is quite spaghetti mainly because of how WifiStateMachine is. \n\nBut I think doing it via a callback may not be ideal either. It seems hiding the problem to me. What if we have a layer that provides interfaces to execute commands like Connecting, Roaming, and etc? WifiConnectivityManager calls into this layer to issue the requests and let it deal with WifiStateMachine and figure out how the requests should be handled.\n\nI will file a bug to track this investigation.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 637,
        "startChar": 16,
        "endLine": 637,
        "endChar": 29
      },
      "revId": "21f7c746784993d3002f9a2da59575a70544ecfa",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
